<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick I of Denmark</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1471">1471</a>–<a href="/years/1533">1533</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-oldenburg">House of Oldenburg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick I was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> from 1523 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> from 1524 until 1533, a younger son of Christian I and duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig-holstein">Schleswig-Holstein</a>. The Danish nobility elected him after deposing his nephew Christian II. He tolerated the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a>, and in 1532 he captured Christian II, who was imprisoned for the rest of his life.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-i-of-denmark.jpg" alt="Frederick I of Denmark" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Jacob Binck · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frederik1dk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frederick was born on 7 October 1471, the youngest son of King Christian I and Dorothea of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, and belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-oldenburg">House of Oldenburg</a>. Not originally destined for the throne, he was elected co-duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> and Holstein in 1482, with his elder brother, King John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. When he came of age in 1490 the two duchies were divided between the brothers, and he lived chiefly at Gottorf Castle.</p><p>In 1500 he persuaded his brother to conquer Dithmarschen. The expedition failed at the Battle of Hemmingstedt, where a third of all the knights of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> and Holstein died. When King John died in 1513, Jutish nobles offered Frederick the throne, but he declined, rightly believing that most of the Danish nobility would stay loyal to John's son, Christian II.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In late 1522 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> nobles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, dissatisfied with Christian II's burgher-friendly policies, approached Frederick. The uprising was proclaimed at the Viborg Landsting on 25 January 1523; Frederick allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, made Johan Rantzau his commander and was acclaimed king on 26 March. Christian II fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> on 2 April. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roskilde">Roskilde</a> in August the coronation charter was adopted, restoring noble and clerical privileges, and it is regarded as the most restrictive ever imposed on a Danish monarch. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> surrendered on 6 January 1524 after an eight-month siege. Frederick recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-i-of-sweden">Gustav Vasa</a> as king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in the Treaty of Malmö of 1524, which ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-union">Kalmar Union</a>.</p><p>Risings in 1524 and 1525 demanded Christian II's return. In 1525 Søren Norby invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blekinge">Blekinge</a> with 8,000 men, and Rantzau defeated the rebels. Frederick governed largely from Gottorf and left daily administration to councillors, notably Mogens Gøye. He was formally Catholic and the protector of the Church in his charter, but he let Lutheran preaching spread, appointed the reformer Hans Tausen his chaplain in 1526 when Tausen was threatened with trial for heresy, and from 1527 authorised the closure of Franciscan houses in 28 towns. He married his daughter Dorothea to Albert, duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, in 1526 and allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> in 1528 without joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-league">Schmalkaldic League</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Frederick died at Gottorp on 10 April 1533 and was buried in Schleswig Cathedral. His reign is seen as a period of stability amid religious upheaval, but the balance he kept dissolved at his death and led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counts-feud">Count's Feud</a> of 1534 to 1536, which ended with the accession of his son Christian III and the establishment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> as the state religion. Contemporary accounts differ: his chancellor Wolfgang Utenhof praised him, while the councillor Johan Friis complained of his avarice. The historian Benito Scocozza called him a prisoner of the nobility. His reign also began the custom of naming Danish kings alternately Christian and Frederick.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frederick I of Denmark” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…three Scandinavian crowns formed in 1397 under Queen Margaret I. In 1523 Sweden broke away under Gustav Vasa, and in the same year the Danish nobility deposed the Union king Christian II and elected his uncle, Duke <b>Frederick of Schleswig-Holstein</b>, as Frederick I; Norway accepted him in 1524.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Albert's marriage on 1 March 1526 to Princess Dorothea, daughter of King <b>Frederick I of Denmark</b>, tied Lutheranism to Scandinavia, and he was aided by his brother George, Margrave of Brandenburg-Ansbach.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Haderslev, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. Died in Gottorf Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.25,9.5&amp;year=1511" aria-label="Haderslev on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4758 277 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Frederick I of Denmark"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5208 547h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5208 547h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.25,9.5&amp;year=1511">What was here in 1511? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
